D = {
"Action Options|DMG": "Optional combat actions. **Climb onto a Bigger Creature.** Win a Strength (Athletics) or Dexterity (Acrobatics) contest against the target's Dexterity (Acrobatics) to gain advantage on attacks against it. **Disarm.** Attack roll contested by Strength (Athletics) or Dexterity (Acrobatics), and the target drops the item. **Mark.** Opportunity attacks against the target have advantage until your next turn ends. **Overrun** and **Tumble.** Contested check to pass through a hostile space once. **Shove Aside.** Push 5 feet sideways, with disadvantage.",
"Adamantine Weapons|XGE": "Melee weapons and ammunition made of or coated with adamantine are very good at breaking objects. Whenever such a weapon or piece of ammunition hits an object, the hit is a critical hit. The adamantine version of a melee weapon, or of ten pieces of ammunition, costs 500 gp more than the normal version.",
"Alien Technology|DMG": "A character figuring out technology from another world makes Intelligence checks. A simple item needs two successes and a complex item needs four. On a 9 or lower, that is a failure, a charge or use is wasted, and the next check has disadvantage. A 10 to 14 is a failure. A 15 to 19 is a success. A 20 or higher is a success and the next check has advantage. Four failures can break it.",
"Alternatives to Epic Boons|DMG": "Instead of an epic boon, the DM can reward a 20th-level character with one of two options, which can be given more than once. **Ability Score Improvement.** Raise one score by 2 or two scores by 1 each, up to a maximum of 30. **New Feat.** The player picks a feat, subject to DM approval.",
"Automatic Success|DMG": "Optional rule. A character automatically succeeds on an ability check when its DC is at or below the relevant ability score minus 5. A second option lets a character who can add proficiency succeed automatically when the DC is at or below the ability score. This does not apply to contests, saving throws, or attack rolls. It speeds play but makes results predictable, so set higher DCs if you want risk.",
"Cleaving Through Creatures|DMG": "Optional rule for fights against hordes. When a melee attack reduces an undamaged creature to 0 hit points, leftover damage can carry over. The attacker picks another creature within reach and, if the original attack roll can hit it, applies the remaining damage. If that creature was undamaged and drops to 0 hit points too, repeat the process until there are no valid targets or the damage fails to drop one.",
"Cone [Area of Effect]|XPHB": "A Cone extends from a point of origin in a direction its creator chooses. Its width at any point equals that point's distance from the origin, so it is 15 feet wide 15 feet out. The effect sets its maximum length. The origin point is not in the area unless the creator decides otherwise.",
"Crashing|AAG": "A spelljammer can ram a target by moving into its space and making an attack roll of 1d20 plus proficiency bonus against its Armor Class. On a hit, or automatically if the DM says the crash is unavoidable, the ship and the target both take bludgeoning damage by target size. Large is 4d10, Huge is 8d10, and Gargantuan is 16d10. A target without hit points damages only the ship. The ship stops against a Gargantuan or immovable target.",
"Crew|AAG": "A standard spelljamming ship crew has one captain to give orders, one spelljammer to pilot, and one or more crew members to operate weapons. Extra crew or passengers make the air supply degrade faster. A spellcaster usually charges at least 50 gp per day to operate a spelljamming helm.",
"Cube [Area of Effect]|XPHB": "A Cube extends from a point of origin on any face of the Cube. The effect sets its size, which is the length of each side. The origin point is not in the area unless the creator decides otherwise.",
"Customizing Ability Scores|PHB": "Optional point buy. You have 27 points to spend. Scores cost 8 for 0 points, 9 for 1, 10 for 2, 11 for 3, 12 for 4, 13 for 5, 14 for 7, and 15 for 9. No score can start above 15 or below 8 before racial increases. This lets you build sets like 15, 15, 15, 8, 8, 8 or 13, 13, 13, 12, 12, 12.",
"Customizing Your Origin|TCE": "Optional rules for races with Ability Score Increase. You can apply each increase to a score of your choice, not stacked and not above 20. You can swap languages for others from a standard list. You can swap proficiencies: skill for skill, armor for a weapon or tool, weapon or tool for a weapon or tool. A Custom Lineage gives +2 to one score, a feat, darkvision 60 feet or a skill, and Common plus one language.",
"Cylinder [Area of Effect]|XPHB": "A Cylinder extends from a point of origin at the center of its circular top or bottom. The effect sets the radius of the base and the height. Unlike other shapes, the origin point is included in the area of effect.",
"Diagonals|DMG": "Optional grid rule for realistic distance. The first diagonal square counts as 5 feet and the second counts as 10 feet, and the pattern keeps alternating even if you move straight between diagonal steps. Example: one diagonal (5), three straight (15), then another diagonal (10) is 30 feet of movement.",
"Downtime Activity: Building a Stronghold|DMG": "You must first own land, bought for 100 to 1,000 gp for a small estate or 5,000 gp or more for a large one. Overseeing construction costs gold and days. Abbey, keep, temple: 50,000 gp, 400 days. Guildhall or trading post: 5,000 gp, 60 days. Noble estate: 25,000 gp, 150 days. Outpost or tower: 15,000 gp, 100 days. Palace: 500,000 gp, 1,200 days. Each day you are away adds 3 days.",
"Downtime Activity: Buying a Magic Item|XGE": "Spend at least one workweek and 100 gp. Make a Charisma (Persuasion) check with +1 per extra workweek and +1 per extra 100 gp, up to +10. The total picks the Magic Item Table to roll on, from A at 1 to 5 up to I at 41 or more. Prices run from (1d6 + 1) times 10 gp for common up to 2d6 times 25,000 gp for legendary. Consumables cost half.",
"Downtime Activity: Carousing|DMG": "You spend as if living a wealthy lifestyle. At the end, roll percentile dice and add your level. Results range from jail for 1d4 days or being robbed of 3d6 times 5 gp at the low end, through making an enemy or a romance, to recouping lifestyle costs at 41 to 80. A roll of 81 to 90 adds 1d20 times 4 gp, and 91 to 100 adds 4d6 times 10 gp.",
"Downtime Activity: Carousing|XGE": "One workweek costs 10 gp (lower class), 50 gp (middle), or 250 gp (upper, needs access). Make a Charisma (Persuasion) check. A roll of 1 to 5 makes a hostile contact, 6 to 10 none, 11 to 15 one ally, 16 to 20 two allies, and 21 or higher three. You can hold unspecified allied contacts up to 1 plus your Charisma modifier (minimum 1). Each week has a 10 percent chance of a complication.",
"Downtime Activity: Crafting|PHB": "You need proficiency with the matching tools, plus any special materials or places. Each day you can craft items worth up to 5 gp of market value, using raw materials worth half that. Bigger items progress in 5 gp steps, so plate armor (1,500 gp) takes 300 days. Several proficient crafters can combine effort at 5 gp per day each. While crafting you live modestly for free or comfortably at half cost.",
"Downtime Activity: Crafting a Magic Item|DMG": "You need a formula and must be a spellcaster who can cast any spell the item holds. Minimum level and cost by rarity: common 3rd, 100 gp. Uncommon 3rd, 500 gp. Rare 6th, 5,000 gp. Very rare 11th, 50,000 gp. Legendary 17th, 500,000 gp. You progress 25 gp per 8-hour day, and spell items use one slot of the spell's level per day. Several qualified crafters can combine effort.",
"Downtime Activity: Crafting an Item|XGE": "You need the right tools and raw materials worth half the selling price. Workweeks equal the gold cost divided by 50, and helpers divide the time. A magic item also needs a formula, an exotic ingredient from a creature of fitting CR, and the right tool or Arcana. Time and cost by rarity: common 1 week, 50 gp. Uncommon 2, 200. Rare 10, 2,000. Very rare 25, 20,000. Legendary 50, 100,000. Consumables halve both.",
"Downtime Activity: Crime|XGE": "Spend a week and at least 25 gp scouting. Choose a DC of 10, 15, 20, or 25, paying 50, 100, 200, or 1,000 gp. Make three checks: Dexterity (Stealth), Dexterity with thieves' tools, and Intelligence (Investigation), Wisdom (Perception), or Charisma (Deception). Zero successes means jail and a fine equal to the profit. One means the heist fails but you escape. Two pays half. Three pays full.",
"Downtime Activity: Gaining Renown|DMG": "A character can spend downtime earning renown with an organization by doing minor tasks and socializing. After spending a number of days equal to current renown times 10, the renown increases by 1.",
"Downtime Activity: Gambling|XGE": "Spend one workweek and stake 10 gp to 1,000 gp or more. Make Wisdom (Insight), Charisma (Deception), and Charisma (Intimidation) checks, each against its own DC of 5 plus 2d10. A gaming set proficiency can replace any skill. Zero successes loses the stake and adds equal debt. One loses half. Two wins the stake plus half again. Three doubles it. Each week has a 10 percent chance of a complication.",
"Downtime Activity: Performing Sacred Rites|DMG": "A pious character spends at least 10 days performing sacred rites in a temple of a god they revere. They can lead the rites if they are a priest, or offer sacrifices or assist if not. Afterward the character gains inspiration at the start of each day for the next 2d6 days.",
"Downtime Activity: Pit Fighting|XGE": "Spend one workweek. Make Strength (Athletics), Dexterity (Acrobatics), and a Constitution check with a bonus equal to a roll of your largest Hit Die (not spent). One check can be a weapon attack roll. Each DC is 5 plus 2d10. Zero successes earns nothing. One wins 50 gp, two win 100 gp, and three win 200 gp. Each week has a 10 percent chance of a complication.",
"Downtime Activity: Practicing a Profession|PHB": "Working between adventures lets you live a modest lifestyle without paying 1 gp per day. If you belong to an organization that offers work, such as a temple or thieves' guild, you earn a comfortable lifestyle instead. If you use Performance proficiency, you earn a wealthy lifestyle.",
"Downtime Activity: Recuperating|PHB": "After three days of downtime recuperating, make a DC 15 Constitution saving throw. On a success, choose one result. End one effect that stops you from regaining hit points. Or gain advantage on saving throws against one disease or poison affecting you for the next 24 hours.",
"Downtime Activity: Relaxation|XGE": "Relax for one week while keeping at least a modest lifestyle. You gain advantage on saves against long-acting diseases and poisons. At the end of the week you can end one effect that stops hit point recovery, or restore one reduced ability score. This does not work on harm from a spell or other magic with an ongoing duration.",
"Downtime Activity: Religious Service|XGE": "Spend one workweek at an aligned temple at no gold cost. Make an Intelligence (Religion) or Charisma (Persuasion) check. A 1 to 10 has no effect, 11 to 20 earns one favor, and 21 or higher earns two. A favor can buy help, support, or 50 percent off cleric spellcasting. You can hold favors up to 1 plus your Charisma modifier (minimum 1). Each week has a 10 percent chance of a complication.",
"Downtime Activity: Research|XGE": "You need a library or sage. Spend one workweek and at least 50 gp, then make an Intelligence check with +1 per extra 50 gp, up to +6. A well-stocked library or knowledgeable sages give advantage. A 1 to 5 learns nothing, 6 to 10 gives one piece of lore, 11 to 20 gives two, and 21 or higher gives three. Each week has a 10 percent chance of a complication.",
"Downtime Activity: Researching|PHB": "The DM decides whether information exists, how many days it takes, and any limits such as finding a specific person or book. The DM may call for checks, such as Intelligence (Investigation) for clues or Charisma (Persuasion) for help. Each day of research costs 1 gp on top of normal lifestyle expenses.",
"Downtime Activity: Running a Business|DMG": "Roll percentile dice and add the days spent, up to 30. A roll of 1 to 20 means you pay one and a half times maintenance, 21 to 30 full maintenance, and 31 to 40 half. A roll of 41 to 60 breaks even. Higher results add profit of 1d6, 2d8, or 3d10 times 5 gp. Each unpaid debt gives a -10 penalty to later rolls.",
"Downtime Activity: Scribing a Spell Scroll|XGE": "You need Arcana proficiency, the spell prepared or known, and its material components. A cantrip scroll works at 1st level. Time and cost: cantrip 1 day, 15 gp. 1st level 1 day, 25 gp. 2nd 3 days, 250 gp. 3rd 1 workweek, 500 gp. 4th 2 workweeks, 2,500 gp. 5th 4, 5,000 gp. 6th 8, 15,000 gp. 7th 16, 25,000 gp. 8th 32, 50,000 gp. 9th 48, 250,000 gp.",
"Downtime Activity: Selling a Magic Item|XGE": "Spend one workweek and 25 gp to find a buyer for one item. Make a Charisma (Persuasion) check. A 1 to 10 offers 50 percent of base price, 11 to 20 offers 100 percent, and 21 or higher offers 150 percent. Base prices: common 100 gp, uncommon 400, rare 4,000, very rare 40,000, legendary 200,000. Each week has a 10 percent chance of a complication.",
"Downtime Activity: Selling Magic Items|DMG": "In a city, make a DC 20 Intelligence (Investigation) check to find a buyer, or fail after 10 days. Legendary items and artifacts cannot be sold this way. Roll percentile dice, add a rarity modifier (+10 common down to -20 very rare) and a Charisma (Persuasion) result. Offers range from a tenth of base price at 20 or lower to one and a half times from a shady buyer at 91 or higher.",
"Downtime Activity: Sowing Rumors|DMG": "Spread a rumor for 2d6 days in a village, 4d6 in a town, or 6d6 in a city, without a break, at 1 gp per day. Then make a DC 15 Charisma (Deception or Persuasion) check. On a success, the community's attitude toward the subject shifts one step toward friendly or hostile as you choose. On a failure, the rumor fails and later attempts fail too.",
"Downtime Activity: Training|PHB": "To learn a language or tool proficiency, find an instructor. The DM decides the time needed and any checks. Training lasts 250 days and costs 1 gp per day. After that you gain the language or tool proficiency.",
"Downtime Activity: Training|XGE": "Learning a language or tool takes ten workweeks, reduced by your Intelligence modifier in weeks (a penalty adds nothing). Training costs 25 gp per workweek. Every ten workweeks of training carries a 10 percent chance of a complication, usually involving the teacher.",
"Downtime Activity: Training to Gain Levels|DMG": "Variant rule. After earning enough XP for a new level, a character must train before gaining its class features. Training time and total cost: levels 2 to 4 take 10 days and 20 gp. Levels 5 to 10 take 20 days and 40 gp. Levels 11 to 16 take 30 days and 60 gp. Levels 17 to 20 take 40 days and 80 gp.",
"Downtime Activity: Work|XGE": "Spend one workweek and make an ability check: Athletics, Acrobatics, Intelligence with tools, Performance, or Charisma with an instrument. A 9 or lower pays a poor lifestyle for the week. A 10 to 14 pays modest, 15 to 20 pays comfortable, and 21 or higher pays comfortable plus 25 gp. Each week has a 10 percent chance of a complication.",
"Downtime and Franchise Activity: Explore Territory|AI": "Spend one workweek and 200 gp per franchise rank. Make a Wisdom (Survival) check with +2 for a cartographer or occultant, +1 per extra workweek, and +1 per extra 200 gp, up to +10. A 1 to 5 is a major threat (costs +50 percent) and 6 to 10 a minor threat (+25 percent). A 16 to 20 finds a feature, 21 to 25 customers, 26 to 30 an ally or monster, and 31 or higher an expanded benefit.",
"Downtime and Franchise Activity: Franchise Restructuring|AI": "Spend two workweeks and 100 gp per franchise rank. Make two checks against DC 13 plus franchise rank. First Intelligence (History) or Wisdom (Insight), then Charisma (Deception, Performance, or Persuasion). Bonuses come from certain staff positions, +1 per two extra workweeks, and +1 per extra 100 gp, up to +10. Zero successes raises monthly costs 20 percent for 1 month. One cuts them 10 percent and two cut them 20 percent for 1d4 months.",
"Downtime and Franchise Activity: Headquarters Modification|AI": "The DM approves a change to an existing headquarters feature. The majordomo and one other spend three workweeks and 1,000 gp per franchise rank. Make an Intelligence (Arcana or History) check, then two Strength (Athletics) or tool checks. Zero successes fails with 1,000 gp per rank overrun. One fails with a 500 gp overrun and advantage later. Two succeed with a 100 gp overrun and a minor drawback. Three succeed cleanly.",
"Downtime and Franchise Activity: Marketeering|AI": "Present a plan to the DM, then spend one workweek and 100 gp per franchise rank. Make three ability checks, each against a DC of 2d10 plus 5 rolled separately. Extra gold adds +1 per 100 gp per rank, and an obviator or secretarian adds +1, to a maximum of +5. Zero successes raises monthly costs 20 percent for 1 month. One does nothing. Two cut costs 25 percent for 1 month and three for 2 months.",
"Downtime and Franchise Activity: Philanthropic Enterprise|AI": "Spend one workweek and 50 gp per franchise rank on a cause. One character makes a DM-chosen check, often Charisma, with +1 for a documancer or occultant. A 1 to 5 is a disaster. A 6 to 10 changes nothing. An 11 to 15 earns 200 gp. A 16 to 20 earns 500 gp. A 21 or higher earns a tax break cutting monthly costs 20 percent for 2 months. A 1 to 5 causes a complication.",
"Downtime and Franchise Activity: Running a Franchise|AI": "Done at the end of each month. A player rolls percentile dice and adds the total days characters and staff spent on it. The result changes monthly cost. A 01 to 10 raises it 150 percent. A 31 to 40 raises it 50 percent. A 41 to 50 changes nothing. A 61 to 70 cuts it 100 percent. A 91 or higher cuts it 150 percent. Modifiers add up, and a cut over 100 percent is profit.",
"Downtime and Franchise Activity: Schmoozing|AI": "Spend one workweek and gold per franchise rank: 10 gp for laborers, 100 gp or more for professionals or rivals, 250 gp or more for nobles. Make a Charisma (Persuasion) check, +1 for an obviator and +1 for a related background. A 1 to 5 brings disrepute and disadvantage on later tries. A 6 to 10 gives advantage next time. An 11 to 15 gains a confidante, 16 to 20 a friend, 21 or higher also a favor.",
"Downtime and Franchise Activity: Scrutineering|AI": "Spend one workweek and 100 gp per franchise rank on bribes and materials, after gaining any needed access. Make an Intelligence check, using a fitting skill if the DM allows, with +1 per extra 100 gp (maximum +10) and +1 for a documancer. A 1 to 5 has no effect. A 6 to 10 learns one piece of lore, 11 to 20 learns two, and 21 or higher learns three.",
"Downtime and Franchise Activity: Shady Business Practice|AI": "The DM approves a scheme. Spend two workweeks and 50 gp per franchise rank. Make three checks, each against its own DC of 2d10 plus 5. Zero successes means arrest and a fine of 250 gp per rank. One earns 50 gp per rank. Two earn 100 gp per rank plus a minor benefit. Three earn 150 gp per rank plus a major benefit.",
"Downtime and Franchise Activity: Team Building|AI": "At least two participants spend one workweek and 50 to 250 gp per franchise rank. Each invents a negative link to a partner's bond, then makes a Wisdom (Insight) check. A 1 to 5 brings disadvantage near the partner. A 6 to 10 changes nothing. An 11 to 15 gives advantage on the next activity check. A 16 to 20 adds one memory, 21 or higher two. A memory: bonus action for enhance ability, 10 minutes.",
"Downtime Revisited|XGE": "An alternative downtime system built on rivals and activities. Rivals are NPCs who oppose the characters, each with goals, assets, and plans that advance after each downtime period. Activities usually take a workweek (5 days), with weeks of 7 days and months of 30. Each day needs 8 hours of work and the total span should be no more than twice the required time. Each activity has a complications table.",
"Emanation [Area of Effect]|XPHB": "An Emanation extends in all directions from a creature or object for a distance the effect specifies. It moves with its origin unless the effect is instantaneous or stationary. The origin is not in the area unless the creator decides otherwise.",
"Emrakul's Madness|PSI": "Using the Sanity score rules, a creature with Sanity below 10 may show corruption in stages. Distorted (8 to 9) and Deformed (6 to 7) change appearance only. Disfigured (4 to 5) can grant simple monster traits. Misshapen (2 to 3) can start turning the creature into another monster. Monstrous (1) is barely recognizable and may fuse with others.",
"Encounters at Sea|GOS": "Each day of a voyage, besides checking for hazards, roll a d20. On a 19 or 20 the ship has a random encounter, and both can happen together. Tables are grouped by character level: 1 to 4, 5 to 10, and 11 to 20. Results include sea creatures, other ships, whirlpools tied to the Elemental Plane of Water, and, at higher levels, mysterious islands.",
"Encumbrance|PHB": "Variant that replaces the armor Strength column. If you carry more than 5 times your Strength score in weight, you are encumbered and your speed drops by 10 feet. If you carry more than 10 times your Strength score, up to your carrying capacity, you are heavily encumbered. Your speed drops by 20 feet and you have disadvantage on ability checks, attack rolls, and saving throws using Strength, Dexterity, or Constitution.",
"Equipment Sizes|PHB": "Variant rule. Found armor, clothing, and similar worn items may not fit the wearer, and an armorsmith, tailor, or leatherworker must alter them. The work costs 10 to 40 percent of the item's market price. The DM can roll 1d4 times 10 or set the percentage by how much alteration is needed.",
"Explosives|DMG": "**Bomb.** Thrown 60 feet, DC 12 Dexterity save or 3d6 fire damage within 5 feet. **Gunpowder.** A horn deals 3d6 and a keg 7d6 fire damage within 10 feet, DC 12 Dexterity save for half. **Dynamite.** DC 12 Dexterity save or 3d6 bludgeoning within 5 feet, half on a success. Each extra stick adds 1d6 (max 10d6) and 5 feet (max 20). **Grenade.** Fragmentation is DC 15 Dexterity, 5d6 piercing within 20 feet. Smoke grenades obscure 20 feet.",
"Facing|DMG": "Optional rule. A creature can change facing when it ends a move, or as a reaction when another creature moves. It has front, side, and rear arcs. It can normally target only creatures in its front or side arcs and cannot see into its rear arc, so an attacker there has advantage. A shield's AC bonus applies only against attacks from the front or the shield's side. The DM can give some creatures different arcs.",
"Falling|XGE": "Two optional rules. **Rate of Falling.** From great heights you descend up to 500 feet instantly and another 500 feet at the end of each later turn until the fall stops. **Flying Creatures.** A flier falls if knocked prone or if its speed drops to 0 feet, unless it can hover or magic holds it up. Optionally, subtract its current flying speed from the fall distance before computing damage.",
"Familiars|MM": "Spellcasters able to cast find familiar, such as apprentices, warlocks, and wizards, are likely to have a familiar. It can be a creature from the spell or another Tiny monster, such as a crawling claw, imp, pseudodragon, or quasit. This variant is intended for NPCs.",
"Familiars|VGM": "Spellcasters able to cast find familiar, such as apprentices, warlocks, and wizards, are likely to have a familiar. It can be a creature from the spell or another Tiny monster, such as a cranium rat, crawling claw, gazer, imp, pseudodragon, or quasit. This variant is intended for NPCs.",
"Fear and Horror|DMG": "Optional rules for dread. **Fear.** Against a hopeless threat, a character makes a Wisdom saving throw at a DM-set DC. On a failure the character is frightened for 1 minute and repeats the save at the end of each of their turns. **Horror.** Against something revolting or a dreadful truth, a character makes a Charisma saving throw. On a failure the character gains a short-term or long-term madness.",
"Fear and Stress|VRGR": "Optional rules. **Seeds of Fear.** Up to three fears per character. Roleplaying one can earn inspiration, once per long rest. **Fear.** Frightening events call for a DC 15 Wisdom save or frightened until the end of the next turn. **Stress.** The DM may add 1 to a Stress Score for dire events, and the score is a penalty on attacks, checks, and saves. A calm day lowers it by 1.",
"Flanking|DMG": "Optional rule. A creature can't flank an enemy it can't see, or while incapacitated. When you and an ally are adjacent to an enemy on opposite sides or corners of its space, you both have advantage on melee attack rolls against it. A Large or larger creature flanks if any part of its space qualifies. On hexes, the allies must have 2 hexes between them against Medium or smaller, 4 against Large, 5 against Huge, and 6 against Gargantuan.",
"Friendly [Attitude]|XPHB": "A Friendly creature views you favorably. You have Advantage on an ability check to influence a Friendly creature.",
"Haunted Traps|VRGR": "Haunted traps arise from tragedy or evil. Each gives off an emanation first, and a character with passive Wisdom (Perception) of at least 10 plus the trap's Haunt Bonus notices it and can react before their next turn. Thieves' tools and dispel magic fail. Channel Divinity or remove curse can disarm it, and the trap saves against your spell save DC. A failure disarms it for 24 hours, or for good if it fails by 10 or more.",
"Hero Points|DMG": "Optional rule for epic play. A character starts with 5 hero points at 1st level. At each new level, unspent points are lost and the total becomes 5 plus half the character's level. After a roll but before its result applies, spend one point to add a d6 to an attack roll, ability check, or saving throw, one point per roll. A point can also turn a failed death saving throw into a success.",
"Hitting Cover|DMG": "Optional rule when a ranged attack misses a target with cover. If the roll would have hit without the cover, the object providing cover is struck. If a creature provides the cover and the roll exceeds that creature's AC, that creature is hit.",
}
